{:edb/db-before-t 2595 :edb/db-after-t 2596 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535908 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:25:28.368Z" 13194139535908 true] [17592186228895 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n${e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3$." 13194139535908 false] [17592186228895 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043349/assets/e70e54b6a70cebd480b86baf9d0e1d90bde0b3d9277c982d5d5e77218d0d2f5d.png)" 13194139535908 true] [17592186228895 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function\n\n$$\n{e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}e^{2t} \\cdot e^{-st}dt + ∫_{3}^{∞}1 \\cdot e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}e^{2t - st}dt + ∫_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + ∫_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if\n\n$$\ns \\leq 0\n$$\n\nFor $s > 0$, we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + ∫_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 - s}[e^{(2 - s)t}]_{0}^{3} + ∫_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + lim_(a → ∞)∫_{3}^{a}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{-e^{-st}}{s}) \\mid _{3}^{a} \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(e^{-as} - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot (0 - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0$." 13194139535908 false] [17592186228895 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043349/assets/48965cee8a09df4f637b4d997fdb9c90673e69a90ea1320434f9b471a2fed280.png)\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-s}{[{e}^{(2-s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$" 13194139535908 true] [17592186228904 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ for $f(t) = u(t - 2) + u(t-6)$, where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139535908 false] [17592186228904 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ for $f(t) = u(t - 2) + u(t-6),$ where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139535908 true] [17592186228904 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 0\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t - 2) &= {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 2 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 2 \\\\\nu(t - 6) &= {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 6 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 2 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le t < 6; 2, \\mid t \\ge 6\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}0 \\cdot e^{-st}dt + ∫_{2}^{6}1 \\cdot e^{-st}dt + ∫_{6}^{∞}2 \\cdot e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + ∫_{6}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for\n\n$$\ns \\leq 0\n$$\n\nFor $s > 0$, we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + ∫_{6}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{6}^{a}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{e^{-st}}{s}]_{2}^{6} + 2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{e^{-st}}{s}) \\mid _{6}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} + \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{2}{s} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(e^{-sa} - e^{-6s}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} - \\frac{2}{s}(0 - e^{-6s}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} + \\frac{2e^{-6s}}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} + \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" 13194139535908 false] [17592186228904 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 2\\end{aligned} \\\\ u(t-6) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043349/assets/6ddb2983dec1f3221778a336bb6ef3cce3138476b67d64334ab6660184a79f95.png)\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{6}+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$" 13194139535908 true] [17592186229121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373866 13194139535908 true] [17592186229121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373872 13194139535908 true] [17592186229121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373884 13194139535908 true] [17592186229121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373891 13194139535908 true] [17592186229121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373962 13194139535908 true] [17592186229122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373897 13194139535908 true] [17592186229122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373910 13194139535908 true] [17592186229122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373923 13194139535908 true] [17592186229122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373936 13194139535908 true] [17592186229122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373949 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373867 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1089 "q-340886" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $s>0,$ where $f(t)=u(5-t)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1113 #uuid "0a4cacbc-5afe-5933-a4d7-e3e3664819fe" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373866 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(5-t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(5-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373868 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373869 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373870 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373871 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1041 "field-1" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373870 13194139535908 true] [17592186373867 1110 #uuid "74f47699-2694-5266-a56e-bc96d91cf802" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373868 1034 "-\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-5s})" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535908 true] [17592186373868 1111 #uuid "e21e27ad-34cb-551e-beb0-9fdef7a86088" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373869 1034 "\\frac{1}{s}({e}^{-5s}-1)" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535908 true] [17592186373869 1111 #uuid "cf36838c-ff48-5e53-80de-138e02058a38" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373870 1034 "\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535908 true] [17592186373870 1111 #uuid "717b84c8-0e96-58bf-84bc-1948b3da34ef" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373871 1034 "\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-5s})" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535908 true] [17592186373871 1111 #uuid "dd3ba88f-acfe-516c-814e-29681aa1eba4" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373873 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373879 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1089 "q-340932" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1100 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1113 #uuid "25e532b1-d332-5b0d-bf0b-74f3f071e83b" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373872 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373874 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373875 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373876 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373877 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373878 13194139535908 true] [17592186373873 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535908 true] [